#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205035} :activity/id #uuid "de17f2e1-7eda-5a05-b05f-fc9631097dd2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050884 :topic/id #uuid "6e36fc4f-7ad7-519b-88fa-4bc240d36c28" :topic/math-academy-id 5572} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205035 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205174 :tutorial/content "Some linear equations can have **no solutions.** This means there is no value of $x$ that can be substituted into the equation to give a true statement.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following equation:\n\n$$\nx+1 = x\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to solve this equation. We start by subtracting $x$ from both sides.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =x \\\\ x+1\\,-\\,x & =x\\,-\\,x \\\\ (x-x)+1 & =(x-x) \\\\ 0+1 & =0 \\\\ 1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the final equation, $1=0,$ is always **false**. In other words, there is no value of $x$ that we can substitute to make the equation true. \n\nTherefore, our equation has no solutions." :tutorial/id #uuid "ae552721-c050-5d83-9cfc-347accbc894b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 11390 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1b8a4ffe-a6bc-5748-9925-775d621ef0ae" :step/math-academy-id 476517 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205036}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205036 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204089 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203852 :question/id #uuid "fd07ad0f-548a-5569-84de-d658a88c4b9c" :question/math-academy-id "e-17087"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d6226fdb-ee73-5a78-87d5-9d6a988a9eb4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048213}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476177} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204600} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204601} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297172} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297173} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297175} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297176} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508100}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation With No Solutions"} :step/id #uuid "2a517a1e-2402-5834-92c6-d387d20f3740" :step/math-academy-id 476518 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205037}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205037 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205208 :tutorial/content "In general, suppose we have an equation in the variable $x$ of the form \n\n$$\nax = b,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $a$ and $b$ are constants, and\n\n- $b$ is *not* equal to zero, which we write as $b \\neq 0.$\n\nThen, if $a = 0,$ the equation has **no solutions.**\n\nIndeed, by substituting $a = 0$ into $ax=b,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax & =b \\\\ (0)x & =b \\\\ 0 & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a *contradiction* (i.e., a statement that's always false) because we were told that $b \\neq 0.$ Therefore, the equation has no solution." :tutorial/id #uuid "8a5cc3be-1b56-5e46-8944-f75697b65b59" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13428 :tutorial/title "Conditions for an Equation To Have No Solutions"} :step/id #uuid "a4d784bb-4dc8-51b7-9c57-1caf81eeaf95" :step/math-academy-id 476521 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205038}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205038 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205207 :tutorial/content "Consider the equation in the variable $x,$ given by\n\n$$\n7qx = -5,\n$$\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solutions, what is the value of $q?$\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax = b{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{7q}_{\\large a} \\ x = \\underbrace{-5}_{\\large b}\n$$\n\nIn this case,\n\n$$\na = 7q, \\qquad b = -5.\n$$\n\nWe know that an equation of the form $ax=b$ with $b \\neq 0$ has no solutions when $a = 0.$\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n7q = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7q & =0 \\\\ \\frac{7q}{7} & =\\frac{0}{7} \\\\ q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the equation $7qx = -5$ has no solutions if $q = 0.$\n\n**Note:** The constant $q$ in this problem is sometimes called a **parameter.**" :tutorial/id #uuid "d31382b3-ae61-5a9e-a3bf-53f71f939a5d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13402 :tutorial/title "Problems Involving Unknown Parameters"} :step/id #uuid "15db0382-7d51-5840-b3da-75384a89354d" :step/math-academy-id 476522 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205039}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205039 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204090 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203853 :question/id #uuid "cba9196c-fdc3-594d-a1ad-948cf75bd366" :question/math-academy-id "e-17213"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0e7426e-553d-5f20-811f-15f3a5ea0056" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204596} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204602} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297177} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508071}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving For an Unknown Parameter"} :step/id #uuid "8211e280-4fa1-564c-9189-09c44ee53dde" :step/math-academy-id 476523 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205040}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205040 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204091 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203878 :question/id #uuid "5ee14499-89b0-59a3-9f13-b0a00c0e518d" :question/math-academy-id "e-18853"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba644b27-7245-5b7b-8cd6-2493836a7d03" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477042} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204597} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204603} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297201} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297203} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297204} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477030} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477031} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477039} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508108}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving For an Unknown Parameter - Constants on Both Sides"} :step/id #uuid "16595ce8-f055-561b-9a7f-397923fee1b4" :step/math-academy-id 476526 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205041}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205041 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204092 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203854 :question/id #uuid "36eadc0b-c2b0-5f84-8f39-2b8399932edb" :question/math-academy-id "e-17214"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d63847ef-6360-50b5-b585-9084140b2d67" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051079}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477074} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204598} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204599} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297205} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297206} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477046} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477050} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477060} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477066}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving For an Unknown Parameter - Variables and Constants on Both Sides"} :step/id #uuid "eddfa8ae-b5d6-5ef0-ba45-f6c57207ae16" :step/math-academy-id 476529}] :activity/title "Linear Equations With No Solutions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186204177}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565489} :activity/id #uuid "c337d6eb-3aa0-55bd-86e4-7976b5c91d04" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050884 :topic/id #uuid "6e36fc4f-7ad7-519b-88fa-4bc240d36c28" :topic/math-academy-id 5572} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565489 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204598 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0f08b77-eeef-5fc2-8543-09a2276b46e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203498} {:answer/id #uuid "ed88b415-ec31-5753-9538-94d29a0edac6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203499} {:answer/id #uuid "06e406e9-458f-5b5b-8a76-47ae321cb09d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203500} {:answer/id #uuid "217b4c73-d235-5933-8a9f-ef748ad498ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203501} {:answer/id #uuid "7cc66499-02a0-5560-9357-f491dc4ae661" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203502}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203502} :answer-field/id #uuid "204618ec-8108-58ed-8408-6f51fe3d50c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203497}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c5eb239a-6da7-5d84-946d-c53824d57aa5" :question/math-academy-id "q-287677" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $x,$ given by\n\n$$\n2px=3x+5\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $p?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $ax=b.$ \n\nWe start by subtracting $3x$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2px & =3x+5 \\\\ 2px-3x & =3x+5-3x \\\\ 2px-3x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $x$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(2p-3)x=5.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $ax=b:$\n\n$$\n{{(2p-3)}_{⏟}}_{a}x={{5}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n2p-3=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2p-3 & =0 \\\\ 2p-3+3 & =0+3 \\\\ 2p & =3 \\\\ \\frac{2p}{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ p & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1866355e-5a27-511e-8e09-7bbdcb7e2ecf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565540}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565519 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297202 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "488785de-3552-5c44-b37c-f80db84151a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295682}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295682} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f518425-6bb3-513e-9a2f-cd6d4cd2d157" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e9e21b0c-1002-52c2-8ff0-0958a8c28640" :question/math-academy-id "q-288644" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $v,$ given by\n\n$$\n(k-2)v-3=5\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $av=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k-2)v-3 & =5 \\\\ (k-2)v-3+3 & =5+3 \\\\ {{(k-2)}_{⏟}}_{a}v & ={{8}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\nk-2=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k-2 & =0 \\\\ k & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9195a63d-152b-58e5-95a9-d8c8dcb4ee50"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565540 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297197 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "298bad1f-9367-5a05-95d2-933a09daee2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295501} {:answer/id #uuid "3dad73b7-975d-5246-83b7-9657d612712e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295623} {:answer/id #uuid "a52921f6-35e3-5fd6-831b-5c76a68fe597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296138} {:answer/id #uuid "c10c0188-b833-5221-be57-af73a4dae17b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296286} {:answer/id #uuid "f34978c9-f088-5aab-b438-960ba47ee1ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296559}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296138} :answer-field/id #uuid "aab0add4-30b0-59ee-8400-8a7cc1beaf29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296994}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1328ee8c-fdcc-59c5-a877-6291af207d84" :question/math-academy-id "q-288596" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $y,$ given by\n\n$$\n(q+9)y=-6\n$$\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $q?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ay=b.$ In this case,\n\n$$\na=q+9,\\,b=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\nq+9=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q+9 & =0 \\\\ q & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f4a9ce2d-f2d1-5c3c-aac6-760f7e33d1b4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565519}}] :activity/title "Linear Equations With No Solutions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564936}]}
